lesson: 111
title: The price of one, then the price of many
stars: 0
explain: When things cost the same each, first find the price of one: divide the total price by how many were bought.
explain: Then multiply the price of one by the new number of things.
explain: If 4 pens cost 12 dollars, one pen costs 12 / 4 = 3 dollars, so 7 pens cost 7 × 3 = 21 dollars.
vars: a=name b=name t=thing p=2..6 u=2..9 m=2..12
need: m != p
answer: m * u
exercise: {p} pens cost {p*u} dollars. How many dollars do {m} pens cost?
exercise: {a} paid {p*u} dollars for {p} notebooks. How much would {m} notebooks cost?
exercise: {p} toy cars cost {p*u} dollars in all. Each car costs the same. What is the cost of {m} toy cars?
exercise: At the fair, {p} tickets cost {p*u} dollars. How many dollars will {m} tickets cost?
exercise: {a} buys {p} {t} for {p*u} dollars. At the same price, how much do {m} {t} cost?
exercise: If {p} cupcakes cost {p*u} dollars, how much do {m} cupcakes cost?
test: A shop sells {p} rulers for {p*u} dollars. {a} wants {m} rulers. How much will {a.he} pay?
test: How many dollars must {a} pay for {m} bottles of juice, if {p} bottles cost {p*u} dollars?
test: Kites are on sale: {p*u} dollars buys {p} of them. What is the price of {m} kites?
test: {a} spent {p*u} dollars on {p} comic books, all at one price. {b} buys {m} of the same books. How much does {b} spend?
exercise: {p} pencils cost {p*u} dollars altogether. How much do {m} pencils cost?
exercise: In New York, {a} buys {p} bagels for {p*u} dollars. How much would {m} bagels cost at the same price?
exercise: How much do {m} {t} cost if {p} {t} cost {p*u} dollars?
exercise: {p} cinema tickets in Toronto cost {p*u} dollars in total. What do {m} tickets cost?
exercise: {p} balls cost {p*u} dollars. How many dollars do {m} balls cost?
exercise: {a} in Melbourne pays {p*u} dollars for {p} bottles of water, all at the same price. How many dollars would {m} bottles cost?
exercise: The price of {p} toy trains is {p*u} dollars, and each train costs the same. Find the price of {m} toy trains.
exercise: {a} spends {p*u} dollars on {p.words} sandwiches. How much would {m.words} sandwiches cost?
test: What will {m} apples cost at a market in Vermont, if {p} apples sell for {p*u} dollars?
test: {b} in Auckland gets {p} postcards for {p*u} dollars. {a} wants {m} of the same postcards. How many dollars does {a} need?
test: For {p*u} dollars you get {p} muffins. How many dollars do you need for {m} muffins?
test: Each of {p} board games costs the same, and together they cost {p*u} dollars. How much would {m} of these games cost?
test: {a} from Vancouver paid {p*u} dollars for {p} {t}. How many dollars would {a.he} pay for {m} {t} at that price?
test: Badges cost {p*u} dollars for {p}. At that rate, what do {m} badges cost?
exercise: {p} pens {p*u} dollars, how much for {m} then
exercise: {a} paid {p*u+1}, no wait, {p*u} dollars for {p} notebooks. {m} notebooks how much
exercise: them {t} is {p*u} dollars for {p}. {b} says {m} of em is {m*p*u}. nah, what's the real price
